for every measurable $E$,
$$\mu(E)<\delta\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad\int_E|f|\,d\mu<\varepsilon.$$

Let $n\ge2$ and let $\Omega\subseteq\mathbb R^n$ be a bounded John domain with distinguished point $x_0$ and admissible constant $c_J\ge1$; set $r_0=\frac14\operatorname{dist}(x_0,\partial\Omega)$ and $B_0=B(x_0,r_0)$. There is a constant $M=M(n,c_J)\ge1$ such that for every $x\in\Omega$ there are balls $B_i=B(x_i,r_i)\subseteq\Omega$, $i\ge0$, with:

1. $|B_i\cup B_{i+1}|\le M\,|B_i\cap B_{i+1}|$ for all $i\ge0$;
2. $\operatorname{dist}(x,B_i)\le Mr_i$ for all $i\ge0$, and $r_i\to0$, $x_i\to x$ as $i\to\infty$;
3. no point of $\Omega$ belongs to more than $M$ of the balls $B_i$.

For $x\notin B(x_0,2r_0)$ the chain starts with $B_0=B(x_0,r_0)$ and follows a John curve from $x_0$ to $x$; for $x\in B(x_0,2r_0)$ it is the explicit geometric chain constructed below. The only use of choice is the selection of one John curve for the given $x$ when $x\notin B(x_0,2r_0)$.

Let $n\ge2$, let $\Omega$ be a bounded extension domain, $k\ge1$ and $1\le p<\infty$. Then:

1. if $kp<n$, $\|u\|_{L^q(\Omega)}\le C\|u\|_{W^{k,p}(\Omega)}$ for every $q$ with $\frac1q\ge\frac1p-\frac kn$ (equivalently $1\le q\le\frac{np}{n-kp}$);
2. if $kp=n$, $\|u\|_{L^q(\Omega)}\le C(q)\|u\|_{W^{k,p}(\Omega)}$ for every finite $q$;
3. if $kp>n$, then for every integer $m\ge0$ and every $0<\alpha<1$ with $m+\alpha<k-\frac np$ there is a representative in $C^{m,\alpha}(\overline\Omega)$ with $\|u\|_{C^{m,\alpha}(\overline\Omega)}\le C\|u\|_{W^{k,p}(\Omega)}$; when $k-\frac np\notin\mathbb Z$ one may take $m=\lfloor k-\frac np\rfloor$ and $\alpha=k-\frac np-m$.

